          "case_name": "In re Interest of Borius H.",
          "citation": "251 Neb. 397, 558 N.W.2d 31 (1997)",
          "court": "Nebraska Supreme Court",
          "year": 1997,
          "date_decided": "1997-01-03",
          "holding": "The court reaffirmed that every motion for an ex parte temporary detention order must be supported by an affidavit of a person with knowledge, held that the State must prove the requirements of Neb. Rev. Stat. § 43-254 by a preponderance of the evidence at the detention hearing, and ordered dismissal because the State adduced no evidence at that hearing.",
          "relevance": "Because the State offered no evidence at the detention hearing, the Supreme Court reversed and remanded with orders to dismiss, illustrating dismissal for failure of proof.",
          "quote": "we once again state that all motions for ex parte temporary detention orders must be accompanied by an affidavit of one who has knowledge of relevant facts warranting temporary detention",
